28:46.560 --> 28:50.560
we derive a control function for an abstraction of our system.

28:50.560 --> 28:53.560
But there is always some residual uncertainty,

28:53.560 --> 28:54.560
which means that in general,

28:54.560 --> 28:58.560
we will drift away from our nominal trajectory.

28:58.560 --> 29:00.560
If I have a closed-loop control law,

29:00.560 --> 29:03.560
I know exactly what I do if I start drifting away,

29:03.560 --> 29:06.560
which means that I can get back to the nominal trajectory.

29:06.560 --> 29:10.560
In open-loop, if I start drifting away,

29:10.560 --> 29:14.560
I don't have any mechanism to bring me back

29:14.560 --> 29:16.560
to the nominal trajectory.

29:16.560 --> 29:19.560
So very explicitly, open-loop means that, say,

29:19.560 --> 29:23.560
I want to get to Robin, and I just compute the trajectory,

29:23.560 --> 29:26.560
close my eyes, I start walking where I'm going.

29:26.560 --> 29:28.560
You see, I was going toward.

29:28.560 --> 29:31.560
I was drifting because I was only relying on dead reckoning,

29:31.560 --> 29:34.560
basically my international measurement unit.

29:34.560 --> 29:38.560
I didn't have visual feedback for state measurement.

29:38.560 --> 29:41.560
I was drifting, I had my open-loop thing,

29:42.560 --> 29:45.560
and I didn't have any way to correct where I was going.

29:45.560 --> 29:46.560
Now that I have my eyes open,

29:46.560 --> 29:49.560
I'm constantly measuring where I am with respect to the room,

29:49.560 --> 29:51.560
which means that as I start drifting,

29:51.560 --> 29:53.560
I go back to my nominal.

29:53.560 --> 29:56.560
That's closed-loop control.

29:56.560 --> 29:59.560
So now you might say, well, okay, cool.

29:59.560 --> 30:02.560
Then closed-loop control is pretty useful.

30:02.560 --> 30:04.560
Why would you ever consider solving

30:04.560 --> 30:09.560
for an open-loop control trajectory?

30:09.560 --> 30:11.560
The unfortunate thing of closed-loop control

30:11.560 --> 30:14.560
is that this object being also a function of the state

30:14.560 --> 30:19.560
is a much more difficult object to do computation over.

30:19.560 --> 30:22.560
Open-loop is just a function of time.

30:22.560 --> 30:24.560
Closed-loop is a function of time and state,

30:24.560 --> 30:26.560
so it's a much more complicated mathematical object.

30:26.560 --> 30:29.560
Computationally, much more expensive.

30:29.560 --> 30:32.560
So ideally, we would like to have an algorithm

30:32.560 --> 30:35.560
that has the same computation efficiency

30:35.560 --> 30:38.560
required to solve an open-loop control problem,

30:38.560 --> 30:41.560
but with the same robustness of properties

30:41.560 --> 30:43.560
of closed-loop control.

30:43.560 --> 30:45.560
A good trade-off is represented

30:45.560 --> 30:48.560
by two-step trajectory optimization,

30:48.560 --> 30:53.560
whereby your control is given by the superposition

30:53.560 --> 30:57.560
of a reference open-loop control signal

30:57.560 --> 31:00.560
plus a feedback tracking term.

31:00.560 --> 31:03.560
The feedback tracking term is simply control law

31:03.560 --> 31:05.560
that tries to bring your system back

31:05.560 --> 31:08.560
to the nominal trajectory that you would follow

31:08.560 --> 31:13.560
with the open-loop control if there were no disturbances

31:14.560 --> 31:17.560
by using some feedback mechanism.

31:20.560 --> 31:22.560
And this is basically the architecture

31:22.560 --> 31:23.560
that we use in practice,

31:23.560 --> 31:26.560
and that's the one that we will be using in this class.

31:27.560 --> 31:32.560
The downside you mentioned of the closed-loop

