46:45.560 --> 46:49.560
Now, the question is, I give you initial condition

46:49.560 --> 46:51.560
for x and y and theta.

46:51.560 --> 46:53.560
Final condition for x and y and theta,

46:53.560 --> 46:56.560
compute a trajectory for the simple car

46:56.560 --> 46:58.560
that obeys some input constraints.

46:58.560 --> 47:06.560
For example, linear speed should not be larger than something.

47:06.560 --> 47:08.560
What we're going to do is to use a tool referred to

47:08.560 --> 47:11.560
as differential flatness to solve this problem.

47:11.560 --> 47:13.560
And I'm going to use this example to show you

47:13.560 --> 47:15.560
how useful differential flatness is

47:15.560 --> 47:19.560
for the purposes of trajectory optimization,

47:19.560 --> 47:21.560
even though here we're talking about feasibility,

47:21.560 --> 47:24.560
not so much about optimization.

47:24.560 --> 47:26.560
In particular, the idea is as follows.

47:26.560 --> 47:28.560
And I want to use an example because at first,

47:28.560 --> 47:31.560
differential flatness is not very intuitive.

47:31.560 --> 47:35.560
But with example, I hope it becomes quite intuitive.

47:35.560 --> 47:40.560
So we have the kinematic model for the simple car.

47:40.560 --> 47:47.560
Now, let's assume that we're given the initial condition

47:47.560 --> 47:53.560
for the xy position for the midpoint of the rear wheel axis.

47:53.560 --> 47:58.560
And I give you the final condition.

47:58.560 --> 48:00.560
Okay?

48:00.560 --> 48:03.560
And then I ask one of you to come here

48:03.560 --> 48:07.560
and just draw a curve that connects these two points.

48:07.560 --> 48:09.560
Again, this curve should be smooth.

48:09.560 --> 48:12.560
Don't ask me about smoothness because I'm going to unsay.

48:12.560 --> 48:15.560
Everything in this class is smooth, almost everything.

48:15.560 --> 48:19.560
You give me a smooth curve that joins those two points.

48:19.560 --> 48:21.560
So my question is,

48:21.560 --> 48:33.560
can I find a time history of v and phi,

48:33.560 --> 48:35.560
my two control inputs,

48:35.560 --> 48:39.560
such that this trajectory gets realized?

48:39.560 --> 48:41.560
In general, for a nonlinear system,

48:41.560 --> 48:43.560
if you just draw a trajectory for a subset

48:43.560 --> 48:46.560
of the state variables, it's not true

48:46.560 --> 48:48.560
that that trajectory is indeed realizable,

48:48.560 --> 48:52.560
meaning that I can find a set of control inputs

48:52.560 --> 48:55.560
that ensure that the vehicle can follow that trajectory.

48:55.560 --> 48:57.560
That's not true in general.

48:57.560 --> 48:59.560
But there's a specific class of systems referred to

48:59.560 --> 49:02.560
as differentially flat systems for which this is true.

49:02.560 --> 49:05.560
If this is true, why this is a dramatic simplification

49:05.560 --> 49:09.560
for the purposes of trajectory control?

49:09.560 --> 49:15.560
Because it basically means that you can just compute a curve

49:15.560 --> 49:19.560
for a subset of the state variables

49:19.560 --> 49:23.560
without having to deal with any differential constraints

49:23.560 --> 49:27.560
and then apply a map that allows you to compute

49:27.560 --> 49:30.560
the control inputs that realize that curve.

49:30.560 --> 49:34.560
So if you're not too crazy in how you design this curve,

49:34.560 --> 49:36.560
then the trajectory that you eventually compute

49:36.560 --> 49:40.560
is a high-quality trajectory, and it's computed very fast

49:40.560 --> 49:44.560
because we did not account for any differential constraint

49:44.560 --> 49:49.560
in solving for this curve.

49:49.560 --> 49:51.560
So let's go to this example.

49:51.560 --> 49:55.560
Again, I've given you a trajectory in terms of x, y,

49:55.560 --> 49:56.560
not even theta.

49:56.560 --> 49:59.560
I just gave you x and y.

49:59.560 --> 50:02.560
And its derivatives, x dot, y dot, x double dot,

50:02.560 --> 50:04.560
y double dot, and so on.

50:04.560 --> 50:07.560
So the question is, how do I compute v and phi

50:07.560 --> 50:09.560
such that this trajectory is realized?

50:09.560 --> 50:12.560
Well, I go back to the equations of motion

50:12.560 --> 50:15.560
and look at the following.

50:15.560 --> 50:19.560
x dot is equal to v cosine of theta.

50:19.560 --> 50:23.560
y dot is equal to v sine of theta.

50:23.560 --> 50:27.560
So if I take the ratio between y dot and x dot,

50:27.560 --> 50:33.560
what I get is tangent of theta.

50:33.560 --> 50:37.560
So with this equation, I can already pinpoint exactly

50:37.560 --> 50:40.560
what the corresponding heading would be

50:40.560 --> 50:42.560
for such a trajectory.

50:42.560 --> 50:48.560
Actually, probably here, this would be here.

50:48.560 --> 50:53.560
Okay, so I can reconstruct the other state variables

50:53.560 --> 50:58.560
that are necessarily implied by the trajectory in terms of x, y.

50:58.560 --> 51:02.560
But most importantly, let's look at the control inputs.

51:02.560 --> 51:04.560
So again, by the equations of motion,

51:04.560 --> 51:09.560
we know that x dot is equal to v of cosine of theta.

51:09.560 --> 51:12.560
I mean, this implies that theta is equal to inverse tangent

51:12.560 --> 51:17.560
of y dot over x dot.

51:17.560 --> 51:19.560
So I already know how to express theta

51:19.560 --> 51:22.560
in terms of x, y and its derivatives.

51:22.560 --> 51:26.560
This implies, by looking again at equations of motion,

51:26.560 --> 51:30.560
that the v is going to be implied

51:30.560 --> 51:37.560
as x dot over cosine of theta.

51:37.560 --> 51:40.560
That the control would give rise to,

51:40.560 --> 51:42.560
as a function of time, to this trajectory.

51:42.560 --> 51:45.560
If cosine of theta is equal to zero,

51:45.560 --> 51:48.560
then you will simply use the other equation,

51:48.560 --> 51:50.560
y dot equals v sine of theta.

51:50.560 --> 51:53.560
Since cosine of theta and sine of theta cannot be zero

51:53.560 --> 51:57.560
at the same time, you can always find a relation between x and y,

51:57.560 --> 52:00.560
x dot and y dot, with the v that would realize

52:00.560 --> 52:02.560
that x and y and x dot and y dot.

52:02.560 --> 52:06.560
Finally, there is another input, which is phi,

52:06.560 --> 52:08.560
the steering angle for the front wheel.

52:08.560 --> 52:13.560
Again, we go back to the equations of motion.

52:13.560 --> 52:24.560
We have that theta dot is equal to v over l tangent of phi.

52:24.560 --> 52:31.560
I have already computed theta as a function of x dot, y dot and x dot.

52:31.560 --> 52:33.560
So I can also compute theta dot.

52:33.560 --> 52:36.560
I would just take the derivative of this relation.

52:36.560 --> 52:40.560
Of course, I would get a second derivative of x and y.

52:40.560 --> 52:44.560
But as I said, once you give me x and y and you tell me that it's smooth,

52:44.560 --> 52:47.560
I know it's x dot, y dot, x double dot and y double dot.

52:47.560 --> 52:52.560
So simply, then phi is equal to the inverse tangent

52:52.560 --> 53:01.560
of l over v theta dot.

53:01.560 --> 53:07.560
So you saw how we went from trajectories in x, y space,

53:07.560 --> 53:10.560
along with information about x dot, y dot and so on,

53:10.560 --> 53:13.560
to the set of remaining set variables,

53:13.560 --> 53:16.560
and most importantly, controls that will give rise to that trajectory.

53:16.560 --> 53:19.560
That is necessarily paired with that trajectory.

53:20.560 --> 53:25.560
We calculate v as the norm of x dot and y dot?

53:25.560 --> 53:27.560
v as the norm of...

53:27.560 --> 53:29.560
Because then it would be the absolute v, not...

53:29.560 --> 53:30.560
Oh, yeah.

53:30.560 --> 53:34.560
But it comes from the equation of motion as well.

53:34.560 --> 53:36.560
Of course, v squared is equal to...

53:36.560 --> 53:38.560
But v can be a, say, another sign,

53:38.560 --> 53:40.560
versus the other one that would not have a sign.

53:40.560 --> 53:41.560
Right.

53:41.560 --> 53:44.560
That's why I prefer this.

53:44.560 --> 53:46.560
You can assume sign in other ways,

53:46.560 --> 53:47.560
but this...

53:47.560 --> 53:51.560
So the mapping between the subset of variables

53:51.560 --> 53:55.560
that you use to find a curve geometrically

53:55.560 --> 53:57.560
and the set of inputs that realize that curve

53:57.560 --> 54:01.560
is not necessarily unique.

54:01.560 --> 54:04.560
So it's pretty amazing, right?

54:04.560 --> 54:06.560
Yeah, just one second.

54:06.560 --> 54:08.560
I'll get back to you in a second.

54:08.560 --> 54:11.560
So the bottom line is that all of the state variables

54:11.560 --> 54:13.560
and the inputs can be determined

54:13.560 --> 54:17.560
by the trajectory of the real wheels and its derivatives.

54:17.560 --> 54:21.560
In particular, in the jargon of differential flatness,

54:21.560 --> 54:26.560
we refer to the subset of state variables x and y

54:26.560 --> 54:29.560
as the flat output.

54:29.560 --> 54:35.560
In particular, what we just found is that

54:35.560 --> 54:42.560
considering as flat outputs x and y,

54:42.560 --> 54:50.560
we were able to recover the theta

54:50.560 --> 54:56.560
as a function of the flat outputs and its derivatives.

54:56.560 --> 55:02.560
And the controlled signals, v phi, as another...

55:02.560 --> 55:04.560
Beta is a function.

55:04.560 --> 55:07.560
And another function, gamma, of z, z dot...

55:07.560 --> 55:10.560
Actually, z double dot, because to compute the theta dot,

55:10.560 --> 55:12.560
I would need to compute the derivative of this

55:12.560 --> 55:13.560
that depends on x dot.

55:13.560 --> 55:18.560
So I would get second derivatives of x and y.

55:18.560 --> 55:21.560
So by using z as flat output,

55:21.560 --> 55:25.560
I can pinpoint the values of the other state variables

55:25.560 --> 55:27.560
and most importantly, the control inputs

55:27.560 --> 55:31.560
that give rise to this trajectory

55:31.560 --> 55:36.560
through non-linear mappings.

55:36.560 --> 55:38.560
Yep?

55:39.560 --> 55:44.560
What is the property that allows you to...

55:44.560 --> 55:46.560
To say if a system is differentially flat?

55:46.560 --> 55:50.560
Well, last slide, I will address this in the question.

55:50.560 --> 55:53.560
So you have to be patient for the next 20 minutes.

55:53.560 --> 55:57.560
So this was just an example that I used

55:57.560 --> 55:59.560
to convey the notion of differential flatness.

55:59.560 --> 56:01.560
As I said, at first it's not very intuitive,

56:01.560 --> 56:05.560
but I hope this example really clarifies the thing.

56:05.560 --> 56:07.560
The formal definition of differential flatness

56:07.560 --> 56:08.560
is as follows.

56:08.560 --> 56:11.560
We say that the system is differentially flat

56:11.560 --> 56:15.560
if there exists a mapping from the state

56:15.560 --> 56:18.560
and the input and possibly derivative of the input,

56:18.560 --> 56:21.560
but importantly, not from...

56:21.560 --> 56:23.560
Not depending on the derivative of the state.

56:23.560 --> 56:27.560
We refer to this subset of variables as z,

56:27.560 --> 56:29.560
the flat output.

56:29.560 --> 56:32.560
In our case, it will be xy.

56:32.560 --> 56:34.560
So we say the system is differentially flat

56:34.560 --> 56:37.560
if there exists such a mapping with a property

56:37.560 --> 56:41.560
that all the state variables, in our case theta,

56:41.560 --> 56:45.560
and all the control inputs, in our case v and phi,

56:45.560 --> 56:49.560
can be uniquely pinpointed as a non-linear function

56:49.560 --> 56:53.560
of the flat output and its derivatives.

56:53.560 --> 56:57.560
In our case, this is what we have.

56:57.560 --> 57:00.560
So it's exactly basically the generalization

57:00.560 --> 57:02.560
of the example that I just provided.

57:02.560 --> 57:05.560
Or in words, we say that the system is differentially flat

57:05.560 --> 57:07.560
if we can find a set of outputs

57:07.560 --> 57:09.560
that has to be equal in number to the control inputs.

57:09.560 --> 57:11.560
This is not intuitive.

57:11.560 --> 57:13.560
This is a key result that follows from

57:13.560 --> 57:16.560
from the deep theory of differential flatness.

57:16.560 --> 57:18.560
We take this fact as granted.

57:18.560 --> 57:20.560
So the number of flat outputs has to be equal

57:20.560 --> 57:21.560
to the number of inputs.

57:21.560 --> 57:24.560
In our case, we have the two inputs,

57:24.560 --> 57:27.560
speed and steering angle,

57:27.560 --> 57:30.560
and the flat output, so two-dimensional inputs,

57:30.560 --> 57:33.560
the flat output was two-dimensional as well.

57:33.560 --> 57:34.560
This is a key fact.

57:34.560 --> 57:35.560
We take it for granted.

57:35.560 --> 57:36.560
There is a reason for it.

57:36.560 --> 57:38.560
In words, the system is differentially flat

57:38.560 --> 57:40.560
if you can find a set of outputs such that

57:40.560 --> 57:42.560
all states and inputs can be determined

57:42.560 --> 57:45.560
from these outputs without integration.

57:45.560 --> 57:50.560
So basically, I don't have to solve for differential equations.

57:50.560 --> 57:52.560
The mapping here does not entail a solution

57:52.560 --> 57:57.560
to any differential equation.

57:57.560 --> 57:59.560
The reason why it's referred to as differentially flat

57:59.560 --> 58:03.560
is because since I don't have the dependencies on x dot,

58:03.560 --> 58:07.560
the flat output z are differentially independent,

58:07.560 --> 58:10.560
which means that they are not related by differential equations.

58:10.560 --> 58:13.560
For example, here, x and y,

58:13.560 --> 58:18.560
they are not related by differential equations.

58:18.560 --> 58:19.560
That doesn't make sense.

58:19.560 --> 58:25.560
The general definition is not super intuitive.

58:25.560 --> 58:28.560
So in order to appreciate it, understand it,

58:28.560 --> 58:30.560
go back to the example that I gave you,

58:30.560 --> 58:32.560
and map the concepts one to one.

58:32.560 --> 58:34.560
Once you do that, this becomes clear.

58:34.560 --> 58:36.560
Key point here is that I can find, basically,

58:36.560 --> 58:42.560
subsets of states, and then if I just compute a curve

58:42.560 --> 58:44.560
with respect to that subset of states,

58:44.560 --> 58:47.560
I can then find the inputs that realize that curve,

58:47.560 --> 58:50.560
which basically translates a complicated

58:50.560 --> 58:53.560
objective optimization problem into the problem

58:53.560 --> 58:57.560
of finding a path in a lower dimensional subset,

58:57.560 --> 59:01.560
and then using mappings in order to pinpoint the controls

59:01.560 --> 59:03.560
for the true system,

59:03.560 --> 59:06.560
allow the true system to follow that trajectory.

59:06.560 --> 59:07.560
Yeah?

59:07.560 --> 59:08.560
So as you were doing a few examples,

59:08.560 --> 59:10.560
I kind of followed step by step,

59:10.560 --> 59:12.560
kind of bringing it back from this.

59:12.560 --> 59:14.560
What was it about the simple car steering

59:14.560 --> 59:16.560
that allowed us to find that set of outputs?

59:16.560 --> 59:18.560
Right, so this is related to the other question.

59:18.560 --> 59:20.560
What are the structural properties

59:20.560 --> 59:24.560
of a linear system model that allow you

59:24.560 --> 59:28.560
to find a flat output relation?

59:28.560 --> 59:31.560
I'll get to that at the end of the lecture.

59:31.560 --> 59:34.560
But yeah, that's like the million dollar question.

59:34.560 --> 59:36.560
It looks magic, right?

59:36.560 --> 59:39.560
It looks even more magic if we talk a little bit more

59:39.560 --> 59:41.560
about the differential flatness implication.

59:41.560 --> 59:45.560
As I said, what that means is that we can,

59:45.560 --> 59:49.560
so every curve in the flat output space

59:49.560 --> 59:52.560
that is differential enough

59:52.560 --> 59:57.560
corresponds to trajectories in terms of x and u,

59:57.560 --> 01:00:00.560
given by the transformation mappings.

01:00:00.560 --> 01:00:04.560
Which practically means that I can just optimize

01:00:04.560 --> 01:00:06.560
the curve in the flat output space

01:00:06.560 --> 01:00:09.560
without reasoning about the underlying dynamics,

01:00:09.560 --> 01:00:12.560
and then apply those mappings to automatically

01:00:12.560 --> 01:00:16.560
account for the kinematic or dynamic constraints.

01:00:19.560 --> 01:00:21.560
So practically, the reason why this is very useful

01:00:21.560 --> 01:00:24.560
is that for the purposes of trajectory optimization,

01:00:24.560 --> 01:00:27.560
it leads to a very simple three-step algorithm.

01:00:27.560 --> 01:00:29.560
And this is what you would do in problem one

01:00:29.560 --> 01:00:31.560
of the first p-sets.

01:00:31.560 --> 01:00:34.560
Step number one, in general,

01:00:34.560 --> 01:00:36.560
you're given initial and final conditions

01:00:36.560 --> 01:00:39.560
for the state and the input.

01:00:39.560 --> 01:00:40.560
Remember that the underlying problem

01:00:40.560 --> 01:00:42.560
is trying to connect the initial condition

01:00:42.560 --> 01:00:43.560
with the final condition.

01:00:43.560 --> 01:00:46.560
You transform those conditions

01:00:46.560 --> 01:00:49.560
in the conditions for the flat output variables.

01:00:49.560 --> 01:00:52.560
Since the mapping beta and gamma

01:00:52.560 --> 01:00:54.560
are actually one-to-one,

01:00:54.560 --> 01:00:57.560
you can always do this transformation.

01:00:57.560 --> 01:01:00.560
So given initial conditions in terms of x and u,

01:01:00.560 --> 01:01:03.560
you transform those into initial conditions

01:01:03.560 --> 01:01:06.560
for the flat outputs and its derivatives.

01:01:06.560 --> 01:01:09.560
So usually this step is straightforward.

01:01:09.560 --> 01:01:10.560
Once you do that,

01:01:10.560 --> 01:01:12.560
so this should be z, so this is the typo,

01:01:12.560 --> 01:01:14.560
I will fix it.

01:01:14.560 --> 01:01:16.560
So once you do that,

01:01:16.560 --> 01:01:20.560
you build a smooth curve in the flat output space

01:01:20.560 --> 01:01:24.560
by using, for example, polynomial interpolation.

01:01:24.560 --> 01:01:25.560
We'll see a little bit more about

01:01:25.560 --> 01:01:27.560
polynomial interpolation in the next slide.

01:01:27.560 --> 01:01:30.560
The y, yeah.

01:01:30.560 --> 01:01:35.560
We denote the flat output as z, not y.

01:01:35.560 --> 01:01:37.560
So we build a smooth curve,

01:01:37.560 --> 01:01:40.560
and now we're just basically interpolating two points.

01:01:40.560 --> 01:01:44.560
So we can use any technique you want for interpolation.

01:01:44.560 --> 01:01:47.560
In this class, we will use polynomial interpolation.

01:01:47.560 --> 01:01:48.560
We'll see a little bit more about

01:01:48.560 --> 01:01:51.560
polynomial interpolation in the next slide

01:01:51.560 --> 01:01:55.560
and also in your p-sets.

01:01:55.560 --> 01:01:57.560
Once you have completed this trajectory,

01:01:57.560 --> 01:02:00.560
then you just apply the reverse mapping beta and gamma

01:02:00.560 --> 01:02:03.560
to obtain the values as a function of time

01:02:03.560 --> 01:02:08.560
of the remaining state variables and the control inputs.

01:02:08.560 --> 01:02:09.560
So super easy.

01:02:09.560 --> 01:02:12.560
And since you're just doing computation

01:02:12.560 --> 01:02:14.560
and what you're doing that is a bottleneck,

01:02:14.560 --> 01:02:16.560
you're just computing this curve,

01:02:16.560 --> 01:02:18.560
but it's just a polynomial interpolation,

01:02:18.560 --> 01:02:19.560
so it's super fast.

01:02:19.560 --> 01:02:23.560
I mean, you can find 100 hertz, super robust.

01:02:23.560 --> 01:02:25.560
You know, and not just being fast,

01:02:25.560 --> 01:02:27.560
but also having problems that are well-conditioned and so on.

01:02:27.560 --> 01:02:29.560
This stuff usually is well-conditioned,

01:02:29.560 --> 01:02:31.560
so super robust and reliable.

01:02:31.560 --> 01:02:32.560
You don't want, I mean, this,

01:02:32.560 --> 01:02:35.560
the lady who separated a part of your system,

01:02:35.560 --> 01:02:37.560
you don't want your trajectory optimizer to crash saying,

01:02:37.560 --> 01:02:39.560
none, fine.

01:02:39.560 --> 01:02:40.560
Yeah.

01:02:40.560 --> 01:02:43.560
Is the way the curve you create by interpolation

01:02:43.560 --> 01:02:45.560
to create that effect the trajectory?

01:02:45.560 --> 01:02:46.560
What's that?

01:02:46.560 --> 01:02:47.560
Yeah.

01:02:47.560 --> 01:02:50.560
So of course, there are many curves

01:02:50.560 --> 01:02:53.560
that you could use for interpolation.

01:02:53.560 --> 01:02:57.560
So we'll get in a couple of slides on saying,

01:02:57.560 --> 01:02:59.560
okay, so I'm solving the feasibility problem,

01:02:59.560 --> 01:03:02.560
but let's also try to inject some notion of optimality.

01:03:02.560 --> 01:03:05.560
Not pure optimality, because that in general,

01:03:05.560 --> 01:03:07.560
this framework does not allow you to do that,

01:03:07.560 --> 01:03:10.560
but having a trajectory that is usually a good compromise.

01:03:10.560 --> 01:03:13.560
But before that, let's talk even more about

01:03:13.560 --> 01:03:15.560
polynomial interpolation.

01:03:15.560 --> 01:03:22.560
The idea is that we want to join the flat output

01:03:22.560 --> 01:03:24.560
in terms of initial and final conditions.

01:03:24.560 --> 01:03:30.560
One way to do that is to represent each flat output variable

01:03:30.560 --> 01:03:36.560
as some polynomial.

01:03:36.560 --> 01:03:46.560
So d j t can be represented as the sum over i of a i j psi i t,

01:03:46.560 --> 01:03:49.560
where psi i t are basis functions.

01:03:49.560 --> 01:03:54.560
For example, we could have psi 1 t is equal to 1,

01:03:54.560 --> 01:04:01.560
psi 2 t is equal to t, psi 3 t is equal to t squared,

01:04:01.560 --> 01:04:05.560
psi 4 t is equal to t cubed.

01:04:05.560 --> 01:04:10.560
So if you do that, we said that we're doing a cubic interpolation.

01:04:10.560 --> 01:04:15.560
So what you want to solve for are the unknown coefficients.

01:04:15.560 --> 01:04:20.560
Again, this is an effort to keep computation load low.

01:04:20.560 --> 01:04:23.560
Instead of optimizing over the entire function,

01:04:23.560 --> 01:04:27.560
you just optimize over coefficients of a parameterized function.

01:04:27.560 --> 01:04:30.560
The more basis functions you use,

01:04:30.560 --> 01:04:34.560
the larger the optimization space is,

01:04:34.560 --> 01:04:36.560
the higher quality of the solution you get.

01:04:36.560 --> 01:04:39.560
But then incur more computational time trials.

01:04:39.560 --> 01:04:41.560
It seems like from what I've seen,

01:04:41.560 --> 01:04:43.560
you're kind of designed to have pretty good progress.

01:04:43.560 --> 01:04:47.560
Well, I'm not in the mind of, yeah.

01:04:47.560 --> 01:04:50.560
But yeah, you can use other ways to do interpolation.

01:04:50.560 --> 01:04:52.560
This is a very popular way in robotics

01:04:52.560 --> 01:04:56.560
because it's very reliable, quite powerful,

01:04:56.560 --> 01:04:58.560
very interpretable, simple to implement,

01:04:58.560 --> 01:05:00.560
and so on and so forth.

01:05:00.560 --> 01:05:03.560
And it does not entail complex numbers.

01:05:04.560 --> 01:05:07.560
What if your basis functions exceeds

01:05:07.560 --> 01:05:10.560
the dimensions of the problem?

01:05:10.560 --> 01:05:15.560
Then it needs to become an optimization problem.

01:05:15.560 --> 01:05:16.560
So then what do you do?

01:05:16.560 --> 01:05:21.560
As I said, you translate the initial and final constraints

01:05:21.560 --> 01:05:24.560
for the x and u into initial and final constraints

01:05:24.560 --> 01:05:27.560
for your output variables.

01:05:27.560 --> 01:05:31.560
And then you just establish a set of linear equations

01:05:31.560 --> 01:05:36.560
that constraint the value of the interpolation coefficients

01:05:36.560 --> 01:05:38.560
and you solve for them.

01:05:38.560 --> 01:05:40.560
You might solve for them uniquely

01:05:40.560 --> 01:05:41.560
or maybe there is not a unique solution

01:05:41.560 --> 01:05:43.560
and then you have a cost objective

01:05:43.560 --> 01:05:45.560
that you want to minimize for the coefficients.

01:05:45.560 --> 01:05:47.560
Does it make sense?

01:05:47.560 --> 01:05:50.560
So basically what we are saying is that

01:05:50.560 --> 01:05:53.560
we reason in terms of polynomial splines

01:05:53.560 --> 01:05:55.560
and we find the coefficients in such a way

01:05:55.560 --> 01:05:58.560
that this polynomial spline actually obeys

01:05:58.560 --> 01:06:02.560
the initial and final conditions.

01:06:02.560 --> 01:06:06.560
In your p-sets, what you will do is to do cubic interpolation

01:06:06.560 --> 01:06:09.560
in order to compute the trajectory for the model

01:06:09.560 --> 01:06:11.560
we are focusing on in this class,

01:06:11.560 --> 01:06:13.560
which is the unicycle model,

01:06:13.560 --> 01:06:16.560
a dynamically extended version of the unicycle model.

01:06:16.560 --> 01:06:17.560
You see that along the way

01:06:17.560 --> 01:06:20.560
I'm providing additional references if you're interested.

01:06:20.560 --> 01:06:22.560
Any questions here?

01:06:25.560 --> 01:06:27.560
So key points.

01:06:27.560 --> 01:06:29.560
Nominal trajectories can be computed

01:06:29.560 --> 01:06:32.560
in a very computationally efficient way.

01:06:32.560 --> 01:06:37.560
In particular, for example, through polynomial interpolation.

01:06:37.560 --> 01:06:40.560
One aspect that we have glossed over is

01:06:40.560 --> 01:06:43.560
what if, besides the differential constraints of motion,

01:06:43.560 --> 01:06:45.560
so kinematic constraints,

01:06:45.560 --> 01:06:48.560
we have additional constraints?

01:06:48.560 --> 01:06:50.560
How do we account for that?

01:06:50.560 --> 01:06:53.560
So that means that basically we need to transform

01:06:53.560 --> 01:06:56.560
those constraints in x and u

01:06:56.560 --> 01:06:59.560
into constraints for the flat outputs.

01:06:59.560 --> 01:07:03.560
And in general, this transformation may be quite challenging.

01:07:03.560 --> 01:07:07.560
In practice, what people do is to use constraints

01:07:07.560 --> 01:07:10.560
in the flat output space that are good proxy

01:07:10.560 --> 01:07:13.560
for the constraints that you have in x and u.

01:07:13.560 --> 01:07:15.560
Usually, and this is a bit more advanced version

01:07:15.560 --> 01:07:17.560
of differential flatness

01:07:17.560 --> 01:07:19.560
that we're not covering in this class,

01:07:19.560 --> 01:07:21.560
this amounts to providing constraints

01:07:21.560 --> 01:07:24.560
in terms of curvature.

01:07:24.560 --> 01:07:25.560
From an intuition standpoint,

01:07:25.560 --> 01:07:29.560
if you have a curve that does a lot of these things,

01:07:29.560 --> 01:07:31.560
it translates into a curve that requires

01:07:31.560 --> 01:07:33.560
a lot of control effort.

01:07:33.560 --> 01:07:35.560
So for example, if you want to minimize control effort,

01:07:35.560 --> 01:07:43.560
you want to avoid these extreme changes in curvature.

01:07:43.560 --> 01:07:45.560
In general, you can set up this problem

01:07:45.560 --> 01:07:48.560
as an optimization problem with flat output variables

01:07:48.560 --> 01:07:50.560
where you have additional,

01:07:50.560 --> 01:07:52.560
so when you try to find the coefficients,

01:07:52.560 --> 01:07:54.560
you try to not only solve the feasibility problem,

01:07:54.560 --> 01:07:57.560
meaning matching initial and final values, conditions,

01:07:57.560 --> 01:08:00.560
but also some objective.

01:08:00.560 --> 01:08:02.560
An objective that is usually considered

01:08:02.560 --> 01:08:08.560
is the jerk or snap of the trajectory.

01:08:08.560 --> 01:08:11.560
Do you know what a jerk is?

01:08:11.560 --> 01:08:14.560
I mean the jerk in the mathematical sense.

01:08:14.560 --> 01:08:18.560
Yes, so the first derivative is referred to as velocity,

01:08:18.560 --> 01:08:20.560
the second derivative is referred to as acceleration,

01:08:20.560 --> 01:08:23.560
the third derivative is referred to as jerk,

01:08:23.560 --> 01:08:25.560
the fourth derivative is referred to as snap,

01:08:25.560 --> 01:08:29.560
the fifth derivative is referred to as crackle.

01:08:29.560 --> 01:08:32.560
You didn't know about that one.

01:08:32.560 --> 01:08:36.560
So we have, well, you know about velocity and acceleration.

01:08:36.560 --> 01:08:44.560
Then there is a jerk, there is snap, and there is crackle.

01:08:44.560 --> 01:08:46.560
So usually in differential flatness,

01:08:46.560 --> 01:08:51.560
you try to find a trajectory that minimizes snap or jerk,

01:08:51.560 --> 01:08:54.560
which basically means that you try to find a trajectory

01:08:54.560 --> 01:08:57.560
that is nicely smooth.

01:08:57.560 --> 01:08:59.560
You don't have these extreme oscillations,

01:08:59.560 --> 01:09:02.560
because again, those correspond to trajectories

01:09:02.560 --> 01:09:04.560
with a lot of control effort.

01:09:04.560 --> 01:09:07.560
Usually optimizing for a snap

01:09:07.560 --> 01:09:10.560
gives you a feasible trajectory that is good enough.

01:09:10.560 --> 01:09:13.560
And this is, for example, a de facto standard

01:09:13.560 --> 01:09:17.560
to compute trajectories for quadrotors or other vehicles.

01:09:17.560 --> 01:09:20.560
If you see movies about autonomous quadrotors,

01:09:20.560 --> 01:09:26.560
95% they are running this algorithm with snap minimization.

01:09:26.560 --> 01:09:31.560
So super powerful, super useful.

01:09:31.560 --> 01:09:34.560
So in this class, we will consider

01:09:34.560 --> 01:09:36.560
a specific set of constraints, which is, of course,

01:09:36.560 --> 01:09:38.560
which is indeed very useful,

01:09:38.560 --> 01:09:41.560
and a specific technique to address those constraints

01:09:41.560 --> 01:09:43.560
in the context of differential flatness.

01:09:43.560 --> 01:09:47.560
In particular, we consider the case of input bounds,

01:09:47.560 --> 01:09:50.560
meaning how do we transform the constraint

01:09:50.560 --> 01:09:56.560
that your input u has to be less than an upper bound,

01:09:56.560 --> 01:10:00.560
so say velocity less than five meters per second,

01:10:00.560 --> 01:10:04.560
how do we translate that constraint

01:10:04.560 --> 01:10:06.560
into the flat output space?

01:10:06.560 --> 01:10:08.560
The technique that we will see next time

01:10:08.560 --> 01:10:11.560
is referred to as time scaling.

01:10:11.560 --> 01:10:13.560
The idea of time scaling is that

01:10:13.560 --> 01:10:15.560
in order to fulfill this constraint,

01:10:15.560 --> 01:10:17.560
we first solve the problem

01:10:17.560 --> 01:10:20.560
without accounting for that constraint,

01:10:20.560 --> 01:10:23.560
and then we time and re-parameterize the trajectory

01:10:23.560 --> 01:10:25.560
until that constraint is satisfied.

01:10:25.560 --> 01:10:31.560
So basically, we increase the pace or slow down the pace

01:10:31.560 --> 01:10:34.560
at which we move along the trajectory

01:10:34.560 --> 01:10:37.560
until we are satisfied with the control bounds.

01:10:37.560 --> 01:10:38.560
We'll see that next time.

01:10:38.560 --> 01:10:42.560
It will also be reflected in the first problems of your PCET.

01:10:45.560 --> 01:10:47.560
So I hope that now differential flatness is clear

01:10:47.560 --> 01:10:49.560
and you appreciate the power of these methodologies,

01:10:49.560 --> 01:10:52.560
but then it goes back to the equation

01:10:52.560 --> 01:10:54.560
from a couple of your colleagues.

01:10:54.560 --> 01:10:56.560
One is the system is differentially flat.

01:10:56.560 --> 01:10:58.560
Now, you really want to use it everywhere,

01:10:58.560 --> 01:11:00.560
but can you use it everywhere?

01:11:00.560 --> 01:11:02.560
Now, unfortunately,

01:11:02.560 --> 01:11:05.560
the existence of a general computable criterion

01:11:05.560 --> 01:11:08.560
to check whether a system is differentially flat

01:11:08.560 --> 01:11:10.560
does not exist.

01:11:10.560 --> 01:11:12.560
So if you're starting your PhD

01:11:12.560 --> 01:11:14.560
and you want to do a very ambitious PhD,

01:11:14.560 --> 01:11:17.560
that could be a good research project,

01:11:17.560 --> 01:11:19.560
but a very hard one.

01:11:19.560 --> 01:11:21.560
There are actually necessary sufficient conditions,

01:11:21.560 --> 01:11:26.560
but in general, they do not lead to a computable algorithm.

01:11:26.560 --> 01:11:28.560
Only for some cases you can apply it,

01:11:28.560 --> 01:11:29.560
but in general, you can't.

01:11:29.560 --> 01:11:34.560
The reality is that if you have an interesting system

01:11:34.560 --> 01:11:37.560
and you find out that that system is differentially flat,

01:11:37.560 --> 01:11:39.560
you usually write a paper about it

01:11:39.560 --> 01:11:43.560
because it's an important contribution to the literature.

01:11:43.560 --> 01:11:46.560
And there are papers out there, some of them cited here,

01:11:46.560 --> 01:11:51.560
whereby the authors provide a catalog of systems

01:11:51.560 --> 01:11:53.560
that are known to be differentially flat

01:11:53.560 --> 01:11:56.560
and for which the flatness mapping,

01:11:56.560 --> 01:11:58.560
so basically those functions beta and gamma,

01:11:58.560 --> 01:12:02.560
that relates flat outputs with x and u are provided.

01:12:02.560 --> 01:12:04.560
This is, for example, a good reference.

01:12:04.560 --> 01:12:07.560
And there are also more recent ones.

01:12:07.560 --> 01:12:12.560
So here, I give you a little more references for this topic

01:12:12.560 --> 01:12:14.560
if you want to get deeper into this topic.

01:12:15.560 --> 01:12:17.560
In terms of application, differential flatness

01:12:17.560 --> 01:12:21.560
is really an applied mathematics topic.

01:12:21.560 --> 01:12:24.560
So if you're interested in going deeper

01:12:24.560 --> 01:12:27.560
on the topic of differential flatness

01:12:27.560 --> 01:12:29.560
using projective optimization,

01:12:29.560 --> 01:12:34.560
I suggest these two papers from Richard Murray at Caltech.

01:12:34.560 --> 01:12:37.560
This deals more with the nuts and bolts

01:12:37.560 --> 01:12:39.560
of applying differential flatness

01:12:39.560 --> 01:12:40.560
to projective optimization.

01:12:40.560 --> 01:12:43.560
This is more an overview of what differential flatness is

01:12:43.560 --> 01:12:45.560
and provides a catalog of systems

01:12:45.560 --> 01:12:47.560
that are differentially flat, so very useful.

01:12:49.560 --> 01:12:51.560
The book, this is the same book

01:12:51.560 --> 01:12:53.560
that I referenced at the beginning,

01:12:53.560 --> 01:12:56.560
also provides a nice discussion about differential flatness

01:12:56.560 --> 01:13:00.560
for the specific application to wheel out the system.

01:13:00.560 --> 01:13:01.560
So very relevant to us.

01:13:01.560 --> 01:13:03.560
In particular, chapter 11, again,

01:13:03.560 --> 01:13:04.560
is the chapter you should look at.

01:13:04.560 --> 01:13:06.560
From a theoretical standpoint,

01:13:06.560 --> 01:13:10.560
excellent reference is this book from Levine,

01:13:10.560 --> 01:13:12.560
extremely mathematically dense,

01:13:12.560 --> 01:13:15.560
so read this book with caution.

01:13:15.560 --> 01:13:19.560
A slightly less mathematical version of this book

01:13:19.560 --> 01:13:21.560
is the book from Rigato.

01:13:21.560 --> 01:13:23.560
Those are really the books whereby you go

01:13:23.560 --> 01:13:27.560
into the mathematical underpinnings,

01:13:27.560 --> 01:13:29.560
like for example, necessary conditions

01:13:29.560 --> 01:13:31.560
for differential flatness, sufficient conditions.

01:13:31.560 --> 01:13:34.560
All of that for us is beyond the scope of this class.

01:13:34.560 --> 01:13:37.560
For our class, what is important to know

01:13:37.560 --> 01:13:39.560
is that the unicycle model,

01:13:39.560 --> 01:13:42.560
the unicycle model that is the one that we use

01:13:42.560 --> 01:13:47.560
for our robot, is differentially flat,

01:13:48.560 --> 01:13:51.560
and the simple car model is differentially flat.

01:13:51.560 --> 01:13:55.560
So for the sake of mobile robotics,

01:13:55.560 --> 01:13:57.560
we are fortunate.

01:13:57.560 --> 01:13:59.560
We can leverage differential flatness.

